hat no bump or group includes; a breaking marker removes the compatibility claim from the opening callout, a change whose changeset was added by a merged pull request (read from the merge commits with one `git log`, from the merge subject `Merge pull request #N` or a trailing `(#N)`) names it as `#N`, and a `Breaking:` marker on a change whose own bump is below what a breaking change needs (major from 1.0.0, minor before) is listed as \"Check the bump\" with the packages and the bump it has. The report also lists every label Callisto manages on the release PR with an `applied` flag: `callisto: ready` when nothing below applies, `callisto: blocked`, `callisto: breaking` (a major bump or a `Breaking:` marker), `callisto: action-required`, `callisto: unreleased-changes`, and exactly one of `bump: major`, `bump: minor` and `bump: patch` for the highest bump. The release PR action creates the applied labels that are missing, adds them, and removes the unapplied ones that are on the PR. Headings in a change's detail are shifted so the shallowest sits at level 5, keeping their relative depth; headings in code fences and indented code are left alone, and one pushed past level 6 becomes a bold line."
docs/architecture/versioning.md:16:- `ReleasePreview` (`crates/callisto-graph/src/release_preview.rs`) is the serializable, versioned view of a plan for people: packages, merged changes, bystanders and findings. Surfaces such as the PR body render it; they do not recompute from the plan. `version --emit-preview` writes it before apply consumes the changesets, and `compose-pr-body --preview` renders from that file. Each changeset change records `pr`, the pull request that added it, read from the first-parent merge commits in one `git log` when the history names one. `ReleasePreview` also carries findings from the changesets (a `Breaking:` or `Action:` marker, a breaking marker whose bump is too low, and no \"backward compatible\" claim when there is a breaking one) and `unreleased`, the publishable packages with source changes since their last tag that the plan does not bump (a package with no tag has no baseline and is not listed) (`version --emit-preview` computes it; a failure to look is a warning). `pr_labels()` derives the managed label catalog, with an `applied` flag per label, from the same findings. `--publish-plan` adds `publish`, the outcome of `plan_workspace_release_offline` on the versioned tree for exactly the packages the preview bumps (the committed decision holds those, not every unreleased package). It never observes a registry or the forge, so the checks that need one (a published version left behind, a name another project owns) are left to the release itself; a refusal becomes a `Stop` finding. `publish.versionedOnly` (optional, absent when empty) names the bumped packages with no publish target, such as a private fixed-group member, which are versioned but never published; the PR body lists them after the publish table. Rows are keyed by the qualified release identity, so `cargo/x` and `npm/x` stay apart.
docs/architecture/versioning.md:17:- `changed_source_files_since_last_tag` lists, per package, the files that differ from its last tag after dropping `DEFAULT_IGNORED_PATHS` (tests, benches, examples, snapshots, Markdown), and `uncovered_changes` returns the changed packages that no pending changeset, nor any member of their fixed or linked group, covers. Neither is wired into the CLI or the PR body yet.

